    "case_id": "25",
    "case_summary": "A 32-year-old man presented to the Department of Periodontology at Tehran University of Medical Sciences with gingival hyperplasia around the lateral and canine teeth of the maxilla on both sides. His medical history was unremarkable. Clinical examination revealed clear vesicular lesions on the attached and marginal gingiva in the canine area, which were asymptomatic. The patient reported no previous dental issues in these areas, and the lesions had gradually appeared over the past two years. No additional lesions or alterations were detected through radiographic and clinical evaluations. Excisional biopsies were performed on both lesions, and the specimens were sent to the Department of Oral Pathology at Tehran University of Medical Sciences.\n\nMicroscopic evaluation revealed gingival tissue covered by parakeratinized squamous epithelium. Deep to the epithelium multiple dilated lymph vessels with different sizes were observed in a little loose fibrovascular tissue. These spaces were lined by a single layer of endothelial cells with flattened nuclei, and contained lymph with a few lymphocytes. Vessels just beneath the surface epithelium filled the connective tissue papilla. Both sides had similar appearance in histologic examination.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
